%S A399900 1,2,3,5,16,26,40,43,54,63,73,78,80,95,100,133,136,143,151,159,166,
%T A399900 168,171,173,200,204,209,268,275,287,295,298,313,323,337,343,349,365,
%U A399900 387,390,406,408,425,451,510,526,544,554,587,590,621,624,627,640,646,655
%N A399900 First elements of the rows of the precise grasshopper infinite barrycade (recursively defined family of permutations of the positive integers having pairwise disjoint sets of partial sums).
%C A399900 A barrycade is a collection of rows whose partial sums never coincide. In the infinite version considered here, every row is a permutation of the positive integers.
%C A399900 The precise grasshopper barrycade is built one row at a time. When a new row is started, its first term is chosen to be the smallest positive integer which has not yet occurred as a partial sum in any earlier row. Thus a(n) is the first term of row n. The rest of the row is filled so that every positive integer eventually appears in that row, while no new partial sum is allowed to coincide with a partial sum from an earlier row. To do this, let k be the smallest positive integer not yet used in the current row. If the current partial sum is s, choose the smallest unused k' != k such that both s+k' and s+k'+k are new, and append k', followed by k. This produces an infinite family of permutations of the positive integers with pairwise disjoint sets of partial sums. Moreover, every positive integer occurs as a partial sum in one of the rows, so the resulting infinite barrycade is break-free.
%C A399900 Equivalently, a(n) is the least positive integer not covered by the partial sums of the first (n-1) rows of the precise grasshopper construction.
%C A399900 The spelling "barrycade" is due to Richard Guy, who used it instead of "barricade" in honor of Barry Cipra, who prompted him to study the problem.
%H A399900 M. Dębski, J. Grytczuk, P. Naroski, B. Pawlik, J. Przybyło and M. Śleszyńska-Nowak, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.18476">Finite and infinite barrycades</a>, arXiv:2609.18476 [math.CO], 2026.
%F A399900 a(n) is the least positive integer which is not a partial sum of any of the first n-1 rows of the precise grasshopper barrycade.
%F A399900 a(n) = min (N \ (S(phi_1) union ... union S(phi_{n-1}))),
%F A399900 where S(phi_i) denotes the set of partial sums of row phi_i, and the rows are generated by the precise grasshopper construction described in the Comments.
%e A399900 The first four rows begin
%e A399900 phi_1 = (1,3,2,5,4,7,6,9,8,11,10,...),
%e A399900 phi_2 = (2,5,1,6,3,8,4,10,7,12,9,...),
%e A399900 phi_3 = (3,6,1,8,2,7,4,11,5,12,9,...),
%e A399900 phi_4 = (5,7,1,6,2,9,3,11,4,13,8,...).
%e A399900 Hence a(1)=1, a(2)=2, a(3)=3, and a(4)=5.
%t A399900 a[n_] := Module[{u, s, p, a, init, extend, covered},covered[0, _] = False;covered[r_, x_] := AnyTrue[Range[r],(init[#]; extend[#, x]; MemberQ[p[#], x]) &]; init[r_] := If[!ValueQ[s[r]], Module[{m = 1}, While[covered[r - 1, m], m++]; u[r] = {m}; s[r] = m; p[r] = {m}; a[r] = m]]; extend[r_, x_] := (init[r]; While[s[r] < x, Module[{k = 1, q = 1}, While[MemberQ[u[r], k], k++]; While[MemberQ[u[r], q] || q == k || covered[r - 1, s[r] + q] || covered[r - 1, s[r] + q + k], q++]; Do[AppendTo[u[r], v]; s[r] += v; AppendTo[p[r], s[r]], {v, {q, k}}]]]);
%t A399900 Table[init[r]; a[r], {r, n}]]
%Y A399900 Cf. A399897-A399909 for other sequences arising from infinite barrycade constructions.
%K A399900 nonn,new
%O A399900 1,2
%A A399900 _Bartlomiej Pawlik_, Sep 16 2026
